use ../lib/CodingRule

from ./GooglePython import GooglePython

export coding-rule GooglePythonExceptions:
    standard: @{GooglePython}
    ruleId: 2.4
    title: Use exceptions with care
    statement:
        Raise built-in exceptions where they fit, such as ValueError for a
        violated precondition. Do not use assert for argument checks or
        logic the program depends on. Custom exceptions inherit from an
        existing exception class and have names ending in Error without
        repeating the module name. Never catch everything with a bare except
        or Exception unless you re-raise or are at an isolation point that
        records the error. Keep try blocks small and use finally for
        cleanup.
    rationale:
        Asserts can be stripped at runtime, so logic that relies on them
        silently disappears. Broad handlers swallow typos, interrupts and
        test failures, and a large try body hides errors raised by lines you
        did not expect to fail.
    exceptions:
        In pytest-based tests, assert is the normal way to check
        expectations. Catching everything is allowed when re-raising or when
        guarding an isolation point such as the outermost block of a thread.
    severity: null
    noncompliant:
        ```python
        \\\
        def set_ratio(ratio):
            assert 0 <= ratio <= 1
            try:
                config = load_config()
                config.ratio = ratio
                save_config(config)
            except:
                pass
        \\\
        ```
    compliant:
        ```python
        \\\
        def set_ratio(ratio):
            if not 0 <= ratio <= 1:
                raise ValueError(f'Ratio must be between 0 and 1, got {ratio}.')
            config = load_config()
            config.ratio = ratio
            try:
                save_config(config)
            except OSError:
                logging.exception('Could not save config.')
                raise
        \\\
        ```
    languages: [python]
    tags: [error-handling, exceptions]
    source: https://google.github.io/styleguide/pyguide.html#s2.4-exceptions
